<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE BYTE-EXACTNESS GATE for proposal 36, channels='1' half (§5, built by B1a).

  This repo has gated render exactness by cmp since the beginning, but only ever
  BETWEEN RUNS — never against a stored file. Proposal 36 rewrites the most
  load-bearing contract in the engine (the frozen page grows a channel
  dimension), and its entire safety argument is that the whole mechanical sweep
  happens while every page is still one channel wide, so the byte gate is green
  BY CONSTRUCTION through the riskiest phase. That argument needs a golden that
  outlives the session which produced it. Hence goldens/mc_mono.wav, committed.

  The fixture project carries one of every path this proposal touches, each in
  its OWN time window so a byte difference names its own culprit by offset:

    0.00-0.60 s   plain clip                 offset 44 .. 115244
    0.65-1.28 s   stretched clip (x1.25)     offset 124844 .. 244844
    1.35-1.95 s   pitched clip (+700 cents)  offset 259244 .. 374444
    2.00-2.60 s   container/asset clip       offset 384044 .. 499244
    2.70-3.30 s   clip on a NESTED lane      offset 518444 .. 633644
    3.35-3.95 s   clip through a twtestclap  offset 643244 .. 758444

  (16-bit stereo PCM, 44-byte header: offset = 44 + frame * 4.)

  It is LOADED into a freshly-new project, never built by a save-load-save round
  trip: §7 trap 10 — load-project deserializes INTO the current project instead
  of replacing it, so a round trip accumulates orphan mixers and chains. Loading
  a fixture into a new project is the shape that is safe.

  Regenerate the .qxp with tests/tools/gen_mc_corpus.qxa. Re-freezing the .wav
  requires an AC that licenses it and a written explanation of why the bytes
  moved (§5).

  ==================================================================
  RE-FROZEN ONCE, at proposal 36 B4 (2026-08-16), under a licence added to
  AC B4.8. The reason lives here, next to the file, because a golden that
  changed needs its explanation where the next reader will be standing.

  WHAT MOVED. Only the last window, 3.35-3.95 s, the one clip that goes
  through a twtestclap insert: offsets 643244..758443 inclusive. Everything
  else — the 44-byte header and the plain, stretched, pitched,
  container/asset and nested-lane windows — is byte-identical to the
  pre-B4 golden. That was checked before freezing, and it is the evidence
  that nothing but the plugin path moved.

  WHY. B4's third bullet re-derives proposal 08's channel-mismatch table
  from PAGE WIDTH instead of bus count. This project is channels='1', and
  tw.test.clap.stereoskew is 2-in/2-out, so the slot now takes MONOFOLD —
  feed the one channel to both plugin inputs, average the two outputs —
  which is exactly what proposal 08 §Layer 3 settled for a stereo plugin on
  a mono wire.

  THE OLD BYTES WERE NEVER A MONO PROJECT'S OUTPUT. Before B4 the project's
  channel count reached no track at all — M1's defining constraint was that
  it must NOT — so every track ran two buses regardless, and the master
  discarded one of them. channels='1' was an inert attribute, not a signal
  path. The golden froze that state. It was never the answer a mono project
  should have given; it was the answer a project whose width did nothing
  gave.

  THE NUMBERS, because "it changed for a good reason" is worth less than an
  arithmetic identity. The fixture's default is out[0] = in[0]*0.5 + in[1],
  out[1] = in[1]*0.5, and both inputs see the same material, so:

      Direct on 2 buses, take bus 0 :  1.5 * g * x     (the old golden)
      MonoFold                      :  0.5 * (1.5*g*x + 0.5*g*x) = 1.0*g*x

  a ratio of exactly 2/3. Measured over the window: 56630 of 57600 samples
  land within 0.333 LSB of old*2/3, i.e. pure 16-bit rounding. The other
  970 are the same finding seen from the other side — the OLD render was
  CLIPPED at full scale there, because 1.5*g*x overflowed 16-bit and
  MonoFold's 1.0*g*x does not.

  DETERMINISM. The replacement was rendered twice, independently, and the
  two renders were byte-identical before either was frozen — so this gate
  is pinned to a deterministic result and not to one sample of a
  nondeterministic one.

  INDEPENDENT SIGNAL. mc_golden_stereo did NOT move. A change confined to
  the mismatch path should leave a channels='2' project alone, because 2->2
  on 2 channels is still Direct, and it did.

  CONSEQUENCE FOR §7 TRAP 14, which B5 must not misread: the two goldens
  were byte-identical to each other, and the proposal treats their
  divergence as a second gate "supposed to break at B5 — when they diverge,
  the sink is real". They diverge NOW, at B4, and the sink is still mono.
  They differ only in this same plugin window, because channel 0 itself now
  differs between a mono and a stereo project. That inference is spent; B5
  needs different evidence.
  ==================================================================

  DEPENDENCY, stated rather than assumed: the last window needs twtestclap.clap
  next to the binary, i.e. the clap submodule present at configure time. Without
  it the plugin is a Missing placeholder, the insert is transparent, and this
  case fails on the very last window while the other five match — which is a
  correct and legible way for a missing dependency to announce itself.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="mc_golden_mono">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <load-project path="../goldens/mc_mono.qxp"/>

    <!-- Pages resident before anything is rendered. Reported, not asserted:
         the count is a function of the readahead and the worker count, so a
         bound would be a flake generator. Making it VISIBLE is the point. -->
    <report-page-memory label="mc_mono after load"/>

    <render filename="mc_mono.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4.0"/>

    <report-page-memory label="mc_mono after render"/>

    <!-- THE GATE. -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="mc_mono.wav" expected="../goldens/mc_mono.wav"/>

    <!-- Every window carries audio, so a golden that went silent could not pass
         by matching a silent render. These bands are wide on purpose: they are
         a liveness check, not a second exactness gate. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_mono.wav"
                         startFrame="2000" frameCount="24000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.60"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_mono.wav"
                         startFrame="33000" frameCount="24000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.60"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_mono.wav"
                         startFrame="66000" frameCount="24000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.60"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_mono.wav"
                         startFrame="98000" frameCount="24000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.60"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_mono.wav"
                         startFrame="131000" frameCount="24000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.60"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="mc_mono.wav"
                         startFrame="162000" frameCount="24000"
                         minRms="0.02" maxRms="0.60"/>

    <!-- AC B1a.2: THE GATE MUST BE SEEN TO FAIL. A gate that has only ever
         passed is not known to be a gate — and the failure mode this guards
         against is not "the verb is wrong" but "the verb compares the render
         with itself", which passes for ever.

         Muting the plugin track is a deliberate, minimal corruption: it changes
         only the last 0.6 s, so the rejection below also demonstrates that the
         diagnosis LOCATES the difference rather than merely announcing one. The
         warning it prints carries the first differing offset (~643 244, the
         start of the twtestclap window) and the differing-byte count. -->
    <set-track-mute trackPath="5" muted="1"/>
    <render filename="mc_mono_muted.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4.0"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="mc_mono_muted.wav"
                           expected="../goldens/mc_mono.wav"
                           expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- …and back. This is not decoration: it says the difference above was the
         mute and not an unrelated drift, and it exercises invalidation across
         the gate rather than only in one direction. -->
    <undo count="1"/>
    <render filename="mc_mono_unmuted.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4.0"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="mc_mono_unmuted.wav"
                           expected="../goldens/mc_mono.wav"/>

    <report-page-memory label="mc_mono after three renders"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <!-- plain, stretched, pitched, asset, folder (holding the nested lane), fx -->
    <assert-track-count equals="6"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
